Consider a visible-light communication (VLC) and visible-light positioning (VLP) system intended to support social-distancing applications, including: (i) indoor localization and navigation of users, (ii) crowd density monitoring, (iii) assistance systems for visually impaired or vulnerable individuals, and (iv) outdoor traffic-control communication between intelligent traffic infrastructure and vehicles. The system uses LED transmitters that modulate intensity to convey data, and photodetector receivers that estimate distance/position from the received signal strength (RSS) of the optical signal.

A fundamental limitation is that ambient illumination and direct or diffuse sunlight act as additive optical noise and shot-noise sources on the VLC channel. Concretely, let the received photocurrent at a receiver be
   y(t) = R · h · x(t) + n_amb(t) + n_th(t),
where x(t) is the transmitted optical intensity, h is the channel DC gain, R is the photodetector responsivity, n_th(t) is thermal/electronic noise, and n_amb(t) is shot noise induced by ambient/solar irradiance whose variance can dominate n_th in outdoor or sunlit indoor settings. RSS-based ranging estimates h (and hence distance) from the time-averaged signal power, so unmodeled or time-varying ambient bias in n_amb directly corrupts position estimates and degrades link reliability.

Problem. Design and analyze a VLC/VLP architecture (jointly specifying modulation/coding, optical filtering, receiver signal processing, and positioning algorithm) that simultaneously achieves all of the following under strong and time-varying ambient-light and sunlight interference:

1. Reliable indoor localization and navigation with bounded position error (e.g., a guaranteed upper bound on RMS positioning error as a function of ambient irradiance level and SNR);
2. Accurate crowd-density monitoring derived from VLP measurements that remains consistent in the presence of ambient noise;
3. A robust assistance-system communication link maintaining a target bit-error rate under ambient interference;
4. An outdoor VLC link between traffic-control infrastructure and vehicles that maintains a specified data rate and BER under direct-sunlight conditions.

Provide rigorous performance guarantees (BER, achievable rate, and positioning Cramér–Rao-type lower bounds) for the proposed scheme as functions of ambient noise statistics, and demonstrate that the scheme attains these guarantees, or otherwise establish fundamental limits showing such guarantees cannot be met.

